description:
File: crate-jmap-chat-server/src/lib.rs:113-130

  /// Register all JMAP Chat method handlers with dispatcher.
  ///
  /// backend is wrapped in Arc so it is cloned cheaply into each handler.
  /// Pass the same Arc<B> to both this function and any application-level
  /// code that needs to access the backend.
  ///
  /// After this call, the dispatcher handles:
  /// Chat/*, Message/*, Space/*, SpaceBan/*, ChatContact/*,
  /// ReadPosition/*, CustomEmoji/*, SpaceInvite/*, and PresenceStatus/*.
  pub fn register_chat_handlers<B>(dispatcher: &mut Dispatcher<B::CallerCtx>, backend: Arc<B>)

The function silently uses Dispatcher::register, which is
documented (jmap-server lib.rs:113) as last-write-wins:
'Registering the same name twice replaces the earlier handler.'

This semantic is correct at the dispatcher level. The problem is
that the chat-server's wrapper doesn't carry the warning forward
to its own caller. A downstream consumer that does:

  register_mail_handlers(&mut dispatcher, mail_backend);
  // ...
  register_chat_handlers(&mut dispatcher, chat_backend);

…will pick up no surprise as long as the two crates' method
namespaces don't collide. But if a future jmap-mail-server starts
registering a 'Message/get' handler (currently it's namespaced
differently, but the workspace's growth is real), or if a
consumer writes their own handler before the register_*_handlers
call and uses one of the names this crate claims, the previously-
registered handler silently disappears. No error, no warning, no
log line.

This is an under-documented contract. Three reasonable fixes:

A. Add a rustdoc warning:
     /// # Caveat: silent override
     ///
     /// This function calls Dispatcher::register for each of the
     /// 38 method names listed above. Per Dispatcher::register's
     /// documented semantics, any pre-existing handler for any
     /// of those names is silently replaced. Callers that want to
     /// compose chat-server with their own handlers MUST register
     /// the per-method handlers AFTER this call, not before.

B. Change the signature to return a Result with a 'name-collision'
   error variant that lists the colliding methods. Breaking change.

C. Provide a 'register_chat_handlers_strict' variant that returns
   an error on collision, leaving the existing function as the
   no-questions-asked path. Non-breaking.

Minimum fix is A. Workspace-shape fix is C if any consumer has
asked for the strict mode; the call sites I can see in the
workspace (jmap-testjig, integration tests) don't, so A is
sufficient until someone asks.

This is the lightest finding in this pass — strictly a documentation
gap. Filing at P3.
